---
// origin: human. Written by Ken, not the pipeline. Static page by design (see /writing/).
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '../../../components/JsonLd.astro';
import { essayBySlug, authorshipOf } from '../../../data/writing';
import { personKenAshe, websiteKenAshe } from '../../../data/schema';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../../consts';

const essay = essayBySlug('i-used-ai-to-learn-ai-then-i-changed-the-plan');
const authorship = authorshipOf(essay);

const title = 'I Used AI to Learn AI. Then I Changed the Plan.';
const description =
  'I asked ChatGPT to quiz me through a 30-day AI course. The useful lesson was a gap between what I had checked and what I was claiming.';
const image = '/images/writing/i-used-ai-to-learn-ai-then-i-changed-the-plan.jpg';

// Article node for this essay. Author and publisher are the canonical Person (imported, not
// forked: DECISIONS.md D9); the essay is human-directed, so unlike Digest posts the author
// is Ken Ashe himself. Same self-contained @graph pattern as every other content page.
const pageUrl = new URL(`/writing/i-used-ai-to-learn-ai-then-i-changed-the-plan/`, SITE_URL).toString();
const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#article`,
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      url: pageUrl,
      mainEntityOfPage: pageUrl,
      image: new URL(image, SITE_URL).toString(),
      datePublished: essay.published,
      author: { '@id': 'https://kenashe.ai/#ken-ashe' },
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://kenashe.ai/#ken-ashe' },
      isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://kenashe.ai/#website' },
      inLanguage: 'en-US',
    },
    websiteKenAshe,
    personKenAshe,
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  image={image}
  ogImageWidth={2816}
  ogImageHeight={1536}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Writing', path: '/writing/' },
    { name: title, path: '/writing/i-used-ai-to-learn-ai-then-i-changed-the-plan/' },
  ]}
>
  <JsonLd slot="jsonld" data={articleSchema} />
  <article class="essay">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/writing/"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Writing</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="essay-header">
      <p class="page-mark" aria-hidden="true">// writing</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta">
        <span class="meta-key">By</span> Ken Ashe
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> September 12, 2026
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> {authorship}
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>I asked ChatGPT for a 30-day AI course that would quiz me, score my answers, and send me back to whatever I could not explain.</p>
      <p>That part worked. I had to produce an answer before I got another explanation. In one exercise I labeled an unchecked answer “accurate,” and the model told me to call it “unverified but plausible” instead.</p>
      <p>Asking it to be strict did not make it right. In this case, it caught a gap between what I had checked and what I was claiming.</p>
      <p>The program was good. I still did not finish it. I wanted the next month pointed at agents that can use tools and complete tasks. The method stayed. The destination changed.</p>
      <figure class="essay-figure">
        <img
          src="/images/writing/i-used-ai-to-learn-ai-then-i-changed-the-plan.jpg"
          alt="Pixel illustration of a fork in the road. On the left, a person works at an AI tutor. The old path is marked finish old plan. The new path is marked agents, if I can check them."
          width="2816"
          height="1536"
        />
        <figcaption>I kept the tutor. I did not finish the plan.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2>Make it test you</h2>
      <p>I was already using AI and building things. I wanted a better grasp of the tools I was using and the decisions I was making.</p>
      <p>My background is finance and project management. I am a CPA and a PMP. I think in requirements, processes, controls, and outcomes. With AI, I wanted a stronger foundation under the practical work.</p>
      <p>The plan was roughly 30 minutes a day. I cared more about understanding the material than finishing on schedule. Mastery, not pace.</p>
      <p>The syllabus covered prompting, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) versus fine-tuning, prompt injection, workflows versus agents, and a few other foundation topics. The method mattered as much as the list.</p>
      <p>I wanted questions that required me to explain an idea or apply it to a small task. Then I wanted feedback that identified what was weak in my answer and why. A score alone would not tell me much, especially if I could not evaluate the reasoning behind it.</p>
      <p>Where something was disputed or uncertain, I needed a source or a practical test, not another confident response.</p>
      <p>The original capstone was a research and publishing system for my site. That gave the lessons a destination.</p>
      <h2>A useful correction</h2>
      <p>The RAG exercise made the method concrete.</p>
      <p>I reviewed an AI-generated explanation of retrieval-augmented generation versus fine-tuning. My assessment included two entries:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Did I verify it? No</li>
        <li>Was the AI answer accurate? Accurate &amp; Unsupported.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>ChatGPT pushed back on the word “accurate.” If I had not verified the answer, the better label was “unverified but plausible.”</p>
      <p>I had admitted I had not checked the answer, then assigned it a label that implied I had established it was correct. The answer might have been right. My assessment still claimed more than my verification supported.</p>
      <p>That distinction matters because I am not only using AI privately. I publish work and explain what I learn. Something can sound reasonable and still need checking before I put my name on it.</p>
      <p>An answer can be accurate and still not be publishable if the source trail is weak.</p>
      <p>That does not mean every sentence needs a research project behind it. It means that when I present a technical claim as established, I should be able to say why I believe it and what supports it.</p>
      <p>There was a second boundary in the same exercise. ChatGPT correcting my label did not independently verify the underlying explanation. It found a weakness in my evaluation. I still needed to check the claims.</p>
      <p>That was a useful role for the tutor: helping me identify a problem in my reasoning without becoming the final authority on the answer.</p>
      <h2>The destination changed</h2>
      <p>By September, I wanted the exercises aimed at agents that can use tools and finish work. This was not a jump from reading to building. The original program already had exercises, and I was already building things. I wanted to change what those exercises were preparing me to do.</p>
      <p>The questions got more operational. What information does an agent need? What should it be allowed to change? When should it ask for approval? How do I know it completed the task rather than simply reported that it did?</p>
      <p>An agent saying “done” is a claim. I want evidence that the requested action happened correctly.</p>
      <p>I wanted to learn about memory, state, logs, and evaluation through the workflows I was trying to build.</p>
      <p>I also want to understand permissions, approvals, failure cases, and cost before giving an agent meaningful authority, especially when wallets or transactions are involved. I do not want an experiment making repeated model calls with no limit on what it can spend.</p>
      <p>The revised direction was more specific: help me build working agents, understand their boundaries, and evaluate their behavior.</p>
      <h2>A bot I did not keep</h2>
      <p>I tested an AI + crypto research bot. I found its output uninteresting and killed it.</p>
      <p>That is the result. I do not have a broader conclusion about whether research bots are useful. I know that this version did not produce something I wanted to keep receiving.</p>
      <p>I could have added sources, topics, agents, or a longer report. Instead I stopped.</p>
      <p>For another version, I would want a clearer answer first: what is this research supposed to help me do? It might support a decision, identify an experiment worth running, or remove a task I would otherwise have to do myself.</p>
      <p>That does not mean every agent needs a business case. I am interested in games and other experiments too. A bot that sends me better dad jokes could be worthwhile simply because I enjoy it.</p>
      <p>Building something to see whether I can is a legitimate exercise. For a bot I expect to use regularly, getting it to run is not enough. I want a reason to keep using it.</p>
      <h2>Learning through the next build</h2>
      <p>I am now building a personal agent system. The roles I have been considering include a chief of staff, an experiment and idea generator, and a dad-joke bot that can use my feedback.</p>
      <p>Those are ideas to implement and test, not capabilities I can already claim.</p>
      <p>The system gives the next round of learning somewhere to land. Instead of studying memory in isolation, I can ask what a particular bot needs to remember. Instead of discussing approvals in the abstract, I can decide which actions require my approval and test whether the boundary holds.</p>
      <p>For each workflow, I want to explain what I asked it to do, what it actually did, where I intervened, and what I would change.</p>
      <p>I also want to separate the agent’s performance from my own understanding. Getting a working result with AI assistance is useful. Being able to explain the design, diagnose a failure, and make an informed change is a different test. A working result does not by itself show how well I understand the system.</p>
      <h2>What I am keeping</h2>
      <p>I am keeping structured learning, quizzes, and practical exercises. I want to answer a question, examine the feedback, check what needs checking, and try again.</p>
      <p>When I have not checked something, I should call it unverified. Then I can decide what evidence I need before relying on it.</p>
      <p>I am also keeping the option to stop a project that is not useful or interesting enough to continue.</p>
      <p>The original program helped me build a foundation and clarify what I wanted to learn next. I do not need to finish every item in the old curriculum to recognize that it was worthwhile.</p>
      <p>For now, I want the lessons attached to the agents I am building. When a system behaves in a way I do not understand, that gives me something to investigate. When it reports success, I want evidence. And when I cannot explain why it worked, I have more learning to do.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

